<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/2nd-century-bce">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of Cynoscephalae</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/197-bce">197 BCE</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/2nd-century-bce">2nd century BCE</a> · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-macedonian-war">Second Macedonian War</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of Cynoscephalae was fought in 197 BCE in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thessaly">Thessaly</a> between the Roman army of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/titus-quinctius-flamininus">Titus Quinctius Flamininus</a> and the Macedonian army of Philip V. In hilly ground and mist, the Roman legions, helped by elephants and by a tribune's attack on the rear of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phalanx">phalanx</a>, overcame the Macedonian phalanx. The defeat ended the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-macedonian-war">Second Macedonian War</a> and forced Philip to give up his possessions in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-cynoscephalae.jpg" alt="Battle of Cynoscephalae" width="320" height="256" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Kryston · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Macedonia_and_the_Aegean_World_c.200.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…Rome the phalanx proved inflexible on broken ground: Philip V was defeated by Titus Quinctius Flamininus at the <b>Battle of Cynoscephalae</b> in Thessaly in 197 BCE, and Perseus' army was destroyed by Lucius Aemilius Paullus at the Battle of Pydna in 168 BCE, when Roman maniples exploited gaps…</blockquote><a href="/empire/antigonid-kingdom-of-macedonia">Antigonid Kingdom of Macedonia · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The Macedonian phalanx fell at <b>Cynoscephalae</b> in 197 BCE and Pydna in 168 BCE, Antiochus III was beaten at Magnesia in 190 BCE, and Carthage and Corinth were destroyed in 146 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/empire/roman-republic">Roman Republic · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Allied with Rome against Philip V in 211 BCE, the Aetolians felt cheated after <b>Cynoscephalae</b> in 197 BCE and invited Antiochus III to Greece.</blockquote><a href="/state/aetolian-league">Aetolian League</a></li></ul></section>
